  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM EST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nThru Sat Night...A reinforcing cold front has pushed into southern \nFL early this afternoon, bringing drier air into the region as \nsurface high pressure slowly pushes across the Gulf and across the\nFL peninsula, ahead of the next developing/approaching low\npressure system. Mostly dry (precip free) conditions will \ncontinue thru this period. WRLY winds this afternoon, 15-20 mph,  \nwith gusts up to 30 mph (35 mph I-4 corridor/Volusia coast). \nBreezy/gusty west winds (gusts 25-30 mph) develop again on Fri, \nbut a little less than today. The pressure gradient relaxes Fri \nnight-Sat night, in proximity to the high pressure center as \nwinds become light and variable to even calm locally. Max temps \nFri/Sat are anticipated to remain below normal, with highs in the \n60s (L70s Martin County). Noticeably cooler overnight with lows \ngenerally in the U40s-M50s (U50s Martin County) Fri-Sun mornings. \nModels now suggesting a few M40s possible overnight/Fri morning \nfor portions of north Lake/NW Volusia.\n\nHazardous beach conditions continue today with rough surf and long \nperiod swells supporting a HIGH risk of rip currents. Heading out to \nthe beach and entering the surf is strongly not advised! Beach \nconditions are forecast to improve gradually through the remainder \nof the week as seas subside and the influence on the tides\nreduces, but will still remain poor to locally hazardous for \nbeachgoers. Always heed the advice of lifeguards.\n\nSun-Wed...Previous Modified...Surface high pressure is kicked \neastward out across the Atlantic waters as a slow-moving cold front \npushes across the southeastern US late this weekend through early \nnext week. Moisture is anticipated to increase across the Florida \npeninsula ahead of the front, with ISOLD-SCT rain chances\nreturning Sun night increasing in coverage Mon-Tue. By early Tue, \nweak low pressure develops along the boundary in the Gulf,  \nfinally pushing the boundary through ECFL as the low lifts across \nthe peninsula and out into the western Atlc, with the highest rain\nchances in the extended occurring on Tue (50-70%). ISOLD lightning \nstorm chances are forecast Mon-Tue, but confidence remains low at \nthis time. Storm hazards will become clearer with time. By Wed, \nthe cold front is forecast to be south of the area, with slightly \ndrier air settling in. Temperatures remain generally slightly \nbelow to near normal areawide, trending warmer across the Treasure\nCoast and slightly above normal again by Wed. Overnight\ntemperatures will remain mostly above normal.",
